we are now joined by civil rights leader  and former u.n ambassador andrew young  he helped draft the civil rights act of  1964 and called  john lewis a friend good morning  ambassador  thank you for being with us  even though i'm older than he was  we all know about john lewis the icon  but  he was your friend tell us about him as  your friend  well john lewis was maybe the quietest  member of the civil rights movement he  could laugh  and he could joke but he was very  seldom rowdy allowed  or he had no ego at all he was  he was he had the power of humility  that just one people over to him  he didn't push he wasn't angry  and he had many reasons to be angry  but he was always dominated by that  loving spirit in his life  that um well  there was a power in his humility  just just made people want to follow him  actually i came from new york  go on sir no i moved from new york  back to atlanta in 1961  after i saw a nashville sit-in story on  television  and i had been  in the south before in pastoring little  country churches  but i thought i was  out of the south until he came on  with the nashville sit-in story and  we realized that our place was back  in atlanta  and i got you to come back in fact and  he said  come home and your wife was like i'm  coming  martin luther king jr's legacy has been  with us since he's passing  do you think it will be the same with  congress congressman lewis's legacy  the generations from now will say  and feel the way they do about him  well i think this generation knows john  lewis  and i think that john lewis  and ct vivian who passed day before  yesterday  will be remembered tact for their  tactical  work uh for their philosophical  conversations  but mostly for that non-violent presence  in the face of severe danger  and the willingness to to march  fearlessly through the valleys of the  shadows  did he in fact understand his own legacy  mr ambassador just how many lives he  touched  i think he did because he touched almost  everybody in the house of  representatives during the 34 years that  he served  there and  he campaigned for them in their own  districts  and he's probably been to every state in  the union  campaigning for members of congress and  that was one of  the reasons why he had so much political  and moral  influence because  they knew that what he said he meant  and that he was willing to live and die  by it  so there was a kind of integrity  that you don't find off  there there is a documentary that is out  now called good  trouble and i know that you are a fan so  we will be hearing more about the  congressman  later on in this show mr ambassador we  thank you  andrew young our former ambassador to  the u.n
 

